## 4. Comparing Login/Registration on Emulator and Real Android Phone

I tested the login flow of an installed application on both an Android emulator and a real Android phone.

| Area             | Emulator                                                      | Real Android Phone                                   |
| ---------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------- |
| Speed            | Performance was generally smooth but depended on the computer | Usually faster and more natural                      |
| UI               | UI looked correct but was viewed through the emulator window  | UI was tested on the actual screen size and hardware |
| Typing           | Used the computer keyboard or emulator keyboard               | Used the real touch keyboard                         |
| Notifications    | May have limitations depending on emulator configuration      | Notifications appeared directly on the actual device |
| Touch Experience | Mouse clicks simulate touch                                   | Real touch gestures give a more realistic experience |

### Two Main Differences

1. **Performance:** The real phone provided a more realistic experience, while the emulator's speed depended on the laptop or computer.
2. **User Interaction:** Typing and touch gestures felt more natural on the real phone compared to using a mouse and keyboard on the emulator.

**Conclusion:** Both environments are useful for testing, but a real device provides a more realistic user experience.

